{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact S1-F head af3348c0ce33e6d097c3e057eb164e4946e487cc on builder/s1f-followups-20261112, all 7 artifacts, for request 17eb790d under promise 00e3230d and review request ed01d419. The head closes the three items that my S1 approval left, as 17eb790d asks. It does not change the overflow key.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- Item 1. The Serving windows section of `docs/security/entry-points.md` now states the creation-table limit with the numbers from my S1 review. The note ties each number to its source constant: `SERVING_WINDOW_CAPACITY = 8192`, the one-hour expiry, `creationAddress: 30`, the overflow cap of `Math.min(10, 30)` and the /64 key of `addressKey`.\n- Item 2. `submit` (`worker.ts:327-331`) now calls `admitServing` for every `found` intent. It passes the bounded copy, or `null` when the intent is over 1 MiB or fails the strict parse, so the oversized value never reaches the fixed owner. `ServingAdmission.admit` (`serving-admission.ts:53-55`) still refuses a creation that fails the bound, and a bounded `found` request whose intent kind is not `found`. A `found` request that fails the bound takes the path that an unverified signature already took: it charges the address and no key. It is rate-limited when the address window is full, and otherwise the decision is `invalid` and the intent goes to its target, which refuses it. Valid and bounded `found` intents behave as before.\n- The new test sends three oversized `found` intents from one address, then one valid `found` intent. The fourth is rate-limited, and the target sees three submits. The note quotes the failure on the unfixed source: line 280 received `refused` instead of `rate-limited`.\n- Item 3. The note explains why a separate creation method would not remove the five-argument call: the reservation check sits inside the shared loop of `account`. Ruling 19a509a5 accepts leaving the call as it is.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. Main a76764a7 is an ancestor, and the pushed bran